  m_Name: complexity
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CampusRift.Learning.LessonData
  id: complexity
  title: Reasoning about Complexity
  topic: Reasoning about Complexity
  titleVN: "T\u01B0 duy v\u1EC1 \u0111\u1ED9 ph\u1EE9c t\u1EA1p"
  pages:
  - title: FIELD NOTES
    titleVN: "GHI CH\xC9P TH\u1EF0C \u0110\u1ECAA"
    contentVN: "\u0110\u1ED9 ph\u1EE9c t\u1EA1p m\xF4 t\u1EA3 m\u1EE9c t\u0103ng t\xE0i nguy\xEAn khi k\xEDch th\u01B0\u1EDBc \u0111\u1EA7u v\xE0o t\u0103ng. C\xF4ng vi\u1EC7c O(n) t\u0103ng tuy\u1EBFn t\xEDnh; O(n\xB2) t\u0103ng b\u1EADc hai. Khi x\xE9t t\u1ED1c \u0111\u1ED9 t\u0103ng ti\u1EC7m c\u1EADn, ta b\u1ECF qua h\u1EC7 s\u1ED1 h\u1EB1ng, nh\u01B0ng th\u1EDDi gian ch\u1EA1y th\u1EF1c t\u1EBF c\xF2n ph\u1EE5 thu\u1ED9c c\xE1ch c\xE0i \u0111\u1EB7t."
    exampleVN: "Duy\u1EC7t n ph\u1EA7n t\u1EED m\u1ED9t l\u1EA7n l\xE0 O(n). Hai l\u01B0\u1EE3t duy\u1EC7t \u0111\u1ED9c l\u1EADp v\u1EABn l\xE0 O(n). So s\xE1nh t\u1EEBng ph\u1EA7n t\u1EED v\u1EDBi m\u1ECDi ph\u1EA7n t\u1EED c\xF2n l\u1EA1i l\xE0 O(n\xB2)."
    takeawayVN: "Big O l\xE0 gi\u1EDBi h\u1EA1n v\u1EC1 t\u1ED1c \u0111\u1ED9 t\u0103ng, kh\xF4ng ph\u1EA3i \u0111\u1ED3ng h\u1ED3 \u0111o th\u1EDDi gian. H\xE3y \u0111\u1EBFm c\xF4ng vi\u1EC7c v\xE0 n\xEAu r\xF5 c\xE1ch bi\u1EC3u di\u1EC5n d\u1EEF li\u1EC7u c\xF9ng c\xE1c gi\u1EA3 \u0111\u1ECBnh."
    content: Complexity describes how resource use grows as input size increases. O(n) work grows linearly; O(n squared) grows quadratically. Ignore constant factors for asymptotic growth, but remember real runtime also depends on implementation.
    example: Scanning n elements once is O(n). Two independent scans remain O(n). Comparing every element to every other element is O(n squared).
    takeaway: Big O is a growth bound, not a stopwatch. Count work and state the representation and assumptions.
    illustration: {fileID: 0}
